---
.title = "Introduction",
.description = "What sqlz is, how it works and where to start.",
.date = .date("2026-11-27T00:00:00"),
.layout = "docs.shtml",
.custom = { "toc": false },
---

sqlz is a SQL toolkit for Zig. You keep writing SQL; sqlz checks it at build
time against a schema rebuilt from your migrations and generates typed Zig
bindings for it. At runtime it executes those queries on SQLite or
PostgreSQL through thin adapters over zqlite and pg.zig.

sqlz is not an ORM. It has no model persistence, relationships or query
builder. Your application keeps control of SQL, connections, transactions,
allocation and domain types.

# [How it works]($heading.id('how-it-works'))

1. **Migrations are the schema.** The checker replays your migration history
   into an in-memory catalog for each configured backend.
2. **Queries are checked on the host.** A normal host program parses each
   query with `libpg_query` (plus a narrow SQLite layer) and checks names,
   types and nullability. Nothing is parsed at comptime.
3. **Bindings are generated into the build cache.** Each query becomes a
   declaration with a typed params struct, a row struct and methods for its
   cardinality.

# [Status]($heading.id('status'))

>[]($block.attrs('warning'))
>sqlz is pre-release. Checking, generated bindings and both runtimes work for
>the current SQL subset. Running migrations against a database (state,
>locking, upgrade and downgrade) is not implemented yet.

The [current-status table](https://github.com/westack-it/sqlz/blob/main/docs/README.md#current-implementation-status)
in the design documents is the authority on what exists today.

